{"sections":[],"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Structure","externalID":"s:21IncidenceMatrixModule0aB0V","modules":[{"name":"IncidenceMatrixModule"}],"remoteSource":{"fileName":"IncidenceMatrix.swift","url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/IncidenceMatrixModule\/IncidenceMatrix.swift#L29"},"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"IncidenceMatrix"}],"title":"IncidenceMatrix","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"IncidenceMatrix","kind":"identifier"}],"role":"symbol","symbolKind":"struct"},"abstract":[{"text":"The unoriented incidence matrix of a hypergraph, an n × m matrix of bits with cell (v, p) set","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"when vertex ","type":"text"},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is a member of column ","type":"text"},{"code":"p","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"@frozen","kind":"attribute"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"IncidenceMatrix","kind":"identifier"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","anchor":"overview","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The vertices are "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<vertexCount"},{"type":"text","text":", and the columns, the hyperedges, are at positions"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<hyperedgeCount"},{"type":"text","text":". The matrix conforms to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hypergraph"},{"type":"text","text":", so the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hypergraphs"},{"type":"text","text":" algorithms run"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"on it unchanged. It is not a "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Graph"},{"type":"text","text":", even when built from one, because a cell edit can give a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"column any number of bits."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A column is any set of vertices. Empty columns and repeated columns are kept. A graph’s"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"self-loop becomes the one bit at its vertex, the hyperedge {v}. "},{"code":"OrientedIncidenceMatrix","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the directed form, and "},{"code":"UndirectedIncidenceList","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is the sparse representation of the same"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"hypergraphs."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"text":"Storage","type":"heading","anchor":"Storage","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Both orientations are stored: n rows of ⌈m\/64⌉ words and m columns of ⌈n\/64⌉ words, with the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"row and column popcounts cached. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"members(ofHyperedgeAt:)"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentHyperedges(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":" are"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"each one "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"BitSet"},{"type":"text","text":" copy of ⌈n\/64⌉ or ⌈m\/64⌉ words, a cell edit writes two bits in O(1), and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"transposed()"},{"type":"text","text":", the dual, swaps the two orientations in O(1). The storage is 2·n·m\/8 bytes, so"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the matrix suits dense or small incidence, such as spectral work or hypergraphs with large"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"hyperedges. Use "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UndirectedIncidenceList"},{"type":"text","text":" for sparse incidence."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Equality compares cell for cell, with equal shape, so column order counts."},{"type":"tex